+# Narrowing a Published List Is Not Search
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+> **Provenance: decided 2026-08-05 by the product owner.** Unlike the 2026-07-31 ADRs in this directory, which were reconstructed from code, this decision was made and recorded on the same day. Its Context and Options Considered record the deliberation as it happened.
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declines free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian: only owners are searchable by typed-in text. On 2026-08-05 the product owner asked for the veterinarian directory to be narrowable to the veterinarians holding one named specialty. Under a plain reading of NG-9 that request is veterinarian search, so the boundary had to be settled before any requirement could be written.
+
+Two facts shaped the reading. The directory already displays each veterinarian's specialties, so the narrowing consumes a value the page itself publishes. And the specialties are a closed list the clinic maintains rather than free-hand text, which NG-3 keeps out of the application.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Hold NG-9 whole** — decline the narrowing. The table stays untouched, and the product owner loses a capability whose entire input is a value the page already shows.
+2. **Withdraw NG-9** — read the request as evidence that search across entities is wanted after all. This reopens pet search and visit search, which nobody asked for, and widens the sample well past what NG-9 was protecting.
+3. **Narrow NG-9, drawing the line at the input rather than at the entity** — free text over any entity but owners stays declined, while selecting the entries of an already-published list that hold a whole value that list already displays is not search.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. Free-text veterinarian search stays declined. Narrowing an already-published directory by an attribute it already displays is in scope, and [`REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004) realizes it.
+
+The test for a future request is the shape of the input, not the entity it targets: text a reader composes is search; a whole value the list itself publishes is narrowing. G-1's demonstration framing holds under either half of that test. Owner search demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating that pattern for veterinarians teaches nothing new, whereas whole-value narrowing is a distinct pattern that adds no new input surface.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `REQ-VET-004` inherits a stated boundary: whole-value matching with letter case disregarded, and prefix matching excluded, so the filter cannot drift into the pattern NG-9 declines.
+- A later request needs this test applied rather than a table row looked up. Prefix matching over specialty names, and a chooser control on the page, both sit on the far side of the line and each need a decision of their own.
+- The narrowing is addressable without being offered — no new place on the page invites a reader to type anything, which is what "adds no new input surface" costs and buys.
+- NG-9 becomes the one row in the Non-Goals table with a decided rather than a derived provenance. The table's blanket framing banner no longer covers it, and says so.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9 — free-text search, narrowed 2026-08-05 to admit whole-value narrowing of an already-published list.
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md § Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — NG-9 as narrowed
+- [`REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004) — the veterinarian directory narrowing this decision permits
+- [`REQ-OWN-002`](../prd.md#req-own-002) — owner search, the one free-text surface NG-9 leaves standing
