+### Published web surface
+
+Routes are internal routing details throughout this application, and are not catalogued here — except where a name is a published contract a caller depends on. The veterinarian directory is the one such case. Its two routes and its narrowing parameter were fixed by the product owner on 2026-08-05, so they are external names rather than an implementation's choice. `docs/prd.md` names no route by its own convention, which leaves this the only place they are recorded.
+
+| Surface | Name | Carries |
+|---|---|---|
+| Veterinarian directory, page form | `/vets.html` | REQ-VET-001, REQ-VET-004 |
+| Veterinarian directory, machine-readable form | `/vets` | REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004 |
+| Narrowing parameter, optional on both | `specialty` | REQ-VET-004 |
+
+Both routes are owned by `VetController`. The parameter is optional; absent, empty, and whitespace-only all yield the unnarrowed directory. A name no veterinarian holds yields the same document with no veterinarian in it, not a failure.
+
+The page form's page links carry the narrowing forward. They are built from parameterized link expressions, which URL-encode the reader-supplied name so it cannot add parameters of its own. Omitting the parameter when no specialty is named is the template's own choice between two link expressions: Thymeleaf renders a null parameter as an empty value rather than dropping it.
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
